specific-volume.interpretation:
The formula states that the specific volume, denoted by \(V_0\), is equal to the inverse of the density, denoted by \(ho\). Density is defined as mass per unit volume (	ext{ho} = m/V
), where \(m\) is the mass and \(V\) is the volume. Therefore, specific volume can be expressed as \(V_0 = 1/ho = V/m\). This means that if a substance has a high density, it will have a low specific volume, indicating that a unit mass of that substance occupies a small amount of space. Conversely, a substance with low density will have a high specific volume. The SI unit for specific volume is cubic meters per kilogram (	ext{m}^3/	ext{kg}
).

specific-volume.limits_and_boundary:
As density (	ext{ho}
) approaches infinity (which is physically impossible for matter but can be a theoretical limit, e.g., in the context of black holes or neutron stars), the specific volume (	ext{V}_0
) approaches zero. This implies that an infinite amount of mass is contained within an infinitesimal volume. Conversely, as density (	ext{ho}
) approaches zero (e.g., for a highly rarefied gas or a vacuum), the specific volume (	ext{V}_0
) approaches infinity, meaning that a unit mass occupies an arbitrarily large volume.
